0 (+0.8 for Black), but a run of heavy losses on moves 11 and 15 (10.1 and 12.8 points) pushed the lead to -14.9. Black's stones built a wall in the middle while White took the edges and the right side, and the later endgame never closed the gap.", "moments": [{"turn": 9, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's F5 lost 5.1 points where the engine wanted F6. White took F6 itself next, and that one exchange moved the game from roughly even (-0.2) to a position where Black needed to catch up."}, {"turn": 11, "text": "D5 was the biggest swing of the opening, losing 10.1 points and taking Black from +0.8 to -9.2. Black had a row of stones along the fifth line, but the engine preferred G4, which would have contested White's G5 stone. Instead Black extended in the middle and left that stone alone."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "F4 was GPT-6.1 Sol's worst move at 12.8 points lost, dropping the lead from -2.0 to -14.9. The engine's choice was H5, which would have pressed on White's H3 and H4 stones. After F4 the right side stayed White's and Black's wall faced the empty middle."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The 20k's D6 gave back 8.5 points, one of its three blunders, moving the score from -12.0 to -3.5 in Black's favour relative to the previous position. It was the best chance to get back into the game, and the engine's B7 would have kept White further ahead."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "White's A7 lost 5.3 points (-15.8 to -10.5), and the engine preferred A4. It was the last real slip in the game, and Black's A4 on the next move changed the score by only 0.2, so the margin stayed near 10 points to the end."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran either read its notes and records, called play/board/choose_opponent with moves it picked itself, or worked on the current position: current-position-liberties.js (inspectLiberties) lists chains and their liberties, the region code counts empty regions and their borders, and the replay code re-plays the completed actual